.SH "NAME"
git-perf \- Inspect Git setup, benchmark status, and summarize a Trace2 run\&.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fIgit perf\fR [\-\-verbose] [\-\-json]
\fIgit perf check\fR [\-\-verbose] [\-\-json]
\fIgit perf bench\fR [\-\-samples \fIN\fR] [\-\-warmup \fIN\fR] [\-\-deadline \fISECONDS\fR] [\-\-verbose] [\-\-json]
\fIgit perf trace\fR [\-\-deadline \fISECONDS\fR] [\-\-verbose] [\-\-json]
\fIgit perf trace\fR \-\-input \fIPATH\fR [\-\-verbose] [\-\-json]
\fIgit perf diagnose\fR [\-\-samples \fIN\fR] [\-\-warmup \fIN\fR] [\-\-deadline \fISECONDS\fR] [\-\-verbose] [\-\-json]
.fi
.sp
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
\fBgit perf\fR inspects repository setup and can measure or trace \fBgit status\fR\&. With no subcommand, it runs \fBgit perf check\fR\&.
.sp
\fBgit\-perf\fR does not write Git configuration itself or select a setting for the user to change\&. The live measurement modes do run Git with the repository\(cqs current settings\&. A configured hook can make arbitrary changes, including changes to Git configuration\&. See EFFECTS below\&.
.sp
\fBcheck\fR reads selected configuration, repository layout, and the index header\&. It does not run \fBgit status\fR, execute an FSMonitor hook, or start the built\-in FSMonitor daemon\&. Child Git processes have optional locks, credential prompts, and ambient trace output disabled\&. Checkout metadata must be regular files of at most 256 KiB each\&. Symlinks, FIFOs, expressions that expand data, and unsupported layouts leave the checkout role unavailable; Git facts collected independently remain available\&.
.sp
\fBbench\fR runs this command with the repository\(cqs current settings:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
git \-\-no\-pager status \-\-porcelain=v2 \-z \-\-branch
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
.sp
The first run after setup is reported separately\&. Warmup runs are excluded from the result\&. If Git, its relevant settings, and the status output remain stable, the measured runs produce a median and median absolute deviation\&. The latter shows how far the run times typically spread from the median\&. A warmup does not prove that Git\(cqs caches or other system activity have stabilized\&.
.sp
Elapsed time starts just before process creation and ends when a blocking waiter observes the child\(cqs exit\&. Pipe draining and cleanup after that point are reported separately\&. Process startup, scheduling, concurrent output parsing and pipe backpressure remain part of the observation\&. Nanosecond units do not imply nanosecond accuracy\&. The configuration comparison covers a selected allowlist; it does not establish that every input to Git remained unchanged\&.
.sp
Live \fBtrace\fR runs that command once with Git Trace2 EVENT capture\&. On supported POSIX systems, git\-perf reads the events through a file descriptor instead of creating a trace file\&. It discards raw events after reducing listed region names to fixed phase names, relative durations, and counts\&. One trace can show where that run recorded time; it cannot show whether the timing is repeatable\&.
.sp
\fBtrace \-\-input\fR reads a regular Trace2 EVENT file, or binary standard input when \fIPATH\fR is \fB\-\fR\&. The parser limits input size, line count, event count, sessions, threads, and nesting\&. This mode does not inspect a repository or run Git\&. Symlinks, devices, FIFOs, compressed files, and archives are not accepted as input files\&.
.sp
The parser supports EVENT format versions 2, 3 and 4\&. Unknown or missing versions produce limited\-quality results\&. Inclusive region durations use Git\(cqs \fBt_rel\fR; exclusive durations subtract immediate child regions on the same thread\&. Wall timestamps place regions on a timeline\&. A disagreement greater than 1 ms between elapsed and wall evidence limits temporal quality\&. This threshold allows for separately sampled clocks and event serialization; it is not a precision guarantee\&. Repeated start or end anchors also limit quality\&.
.sp
\fBactive_wall_ns\fR unions overlapping regions across a root session and its descendants, clipped to the root window\&. \fBaggregate_process_ns\fR instead sums per\-process interval unions and is not CPU time\&. Unrelated offline sessions have no combined wall duration\&. Different phases can overlap, so their wall times must not be added to produce a command total\&.
.sp
\fBdiagnose\fR benchmarks \fBgit status\fR, traces one additional run, and then checks whether the repository or Git executable changed during the diagnosis\&. All three parts share one deadline\&. The traced run is not included in the benchmark median\&. Comparisons use private repository identities, selected Git/config snapshots, and invocation\-local status fingerprints\&. Only comparison states enter the report\&. Drift or missing observations make the combined diagnosis partial while preserving any independently valid benchmark summary\&.
.SH "EFFECTS"
.sp
\fBbench\fR, live \fBtrace\fR, and \fBdiagnose\fR run \fBgit status\fR after printing a warning to standard error\&. They do not ask for a second confirmation\&. Depending on the current Git configuration, an external hook or the built\-in FSMonitor may run, initialized submodules may be inspected, and Git may update its index or optional caches\&.
.sp
\fBgit\-perf\fR does not call \fBgit config\fR to write a setting\&. It cannot make the same promise for a configured hook because a hook is an arbitrary program\&. A hook can make any change that the current user is allowed to make\&.
.sp
git\-perf refuses to run \fBgit status\fR when it detects a partial clone or cannot determine whether the clone is partial\&. Status may fetch missing objects in a partial clone, and this prototype has not verified that it can prevent such a fetch with the selected Git version\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\-\-samples \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Number of measured runs after the first run and any warmups\&. The default is
\fB7\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-warmup \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Number of extra runs omitted from the timings after the first run\&. The default is
\fB1\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-deadline \fISECONDS\fR
.RS 4
Deadline for setup, Git runs, and follow\-up checks\&. The default is
\fB60 seconds\fR
for
\fBbench\fR
and
\fBdiagnose\fR, and
\fB30 seconds\fR
for live
\fBtrace\fR\&. Process cleanup and writing the final report may finish after the deadline\&. Metadata reads are size/type bounded and check the shared deadline\&. This is not a hard real\-time limit on a stalled filesystem operation\&. If process or reader cleanup remains uncertain, no further Git commands are scheduled\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-input \fIPATH\fR
.RS 4
Read an existing Trace2 EVENT stream instead of running Git\&.
\fB\-\fR
selects binary standard input\&. This option cannot be combined with
\fB\-\-deadline\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-verbose
.RS 4
Show every fact used in the report and the limits on how it was collected\&. This option does not collect more information\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-json
.RS 4
Write one experimental JSON document to standard output\&.
\fB\-\-verbose\fR
does not change JSON output\&. Schema version 2 distinguishes the corrected wall\-time fields; workload version 2 uses child\-exit timing instead of reader completion\&.
.RE
.SH "PRIVACY AND INTERPRETATION"
.sp
The human and JSON reports omit raw child\-process output, filenames, repository paths, remote identities, raw Trace2 events, process IDs, session IDs, wall\-clock timestamps, and Trace2 labels that are not explicitly listed in the parser\&. Unknown phase labels count as \fBother\fR\&. git\-perf must read some path\-bearing Git output to find the repository and index, but it discards those values before building the report\&.
.sp
Benchmark timings apply to this checkout, machine, and invocation\&. A trace shows one run and adds overhead that git\-perf does not measure\&. Use these results to choose the next measurement, not as proof that a configuration change will improve performance\&.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
\fB0\fR
.RS 4
A complete or partial report was written\&. This status is also returned if the consumer closes the pipe while reading a successful report\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB2\fR
.RS 4
Arguments were invalid, or git\-perf could not produce the requested report\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB130\fR
.RS 4
Interrupted by
\fBSIGINT\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB143\fR
.RS 4
git\-perf caught
\fBSIGTERM\fR
and attempted to clean up its child process\&.
.RE
